  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nNorthwest flow will lessen tonight under weak ridging. Some\ntrapped moisture aloft in this northwest flow could continue\nsome low clouds through the evening, with some partial clearing\nexpected tonight. This partial clearing will influence low\ntemperatures tonight, with lows generally in the 40s. Some clear\nareas with light winds could see lows into the 30s, perhaps\nbringing isolated frost. As a surface low develops to our\nwest, light winds will become a steady southeasterly flow later\ntonight especially in the west. Inverted surface trough then\nmoves through Wednesday, combined with a weak mid level\nwave/warm front then trailing cold front. The result will be \nsome chances for showers mainly central, north, and east. Some \nweak and elevated instability could provide for a few isolated \nthunderstorms as well on Wednesday. Shear is high Wednesday, but\nwill likely not be full accessible given the elevated nature of\nany thunderstorms that develop. Slightly warmer temperatures \nwill also be found on Wednesday, especially in the southwest, \nwith highs generally forecast in the 60s north and east to 70s \nelsewhere. Breezy southerly winds may also be found as well at\ntimes on Wednesday. Cold front moves south out of Canada\nfollowed by a surface high Wednesday night into Thursday \nmorning. This will bring cooler lows in the mid 30s to mid 40s. \nDepending on winds and cloud cover some patchy frost is \npossible, especially across the north.\n\nSurface high will bring mainly dry conditions on Thursday,\nalthough a stalled front and return flow could bring some\nshowers and thunderstorms to the southwest. Most of the\nprecipitation is expected to remain to our south Thursday,\nperhaps clipping some southern portions Thursday night. Winds \nwill again be easterly, and could be breezy in the southwest. \nCooler highs in the 60s to near 70 can be expected behind \nWednesday nights cold front. Abundant moisture combined with a \nbroad upper low could then bring widespread shower and \nthunderstorm chances of Friday. How far north and east this \nplume of moisture and the upper low goes will determine where \nthe precipitation sets up. Current PoPs favor the northwest and \nsome southern portions of the state for Friday. Temperatures \nwarm slightly for Friday, and generally in the 70s. Broad upper \nlow and trough slowly moves into the area this weekend keeping \ntemperatures near to slightly below normal. As a result broad \nchances for showers and thunderstorms will be across the state \nthrough the weekend. Actual location of precipitation will be \nhighly dependent on intrusion of dry and limited synoptic lift \nat times. Even so NBM still showing 40 to 60% chance for 0.5\" or\ngreater of rain through the weekend, mainly along and west of \nHighway 83. Limited instability and shear should also limit the \nthunderstorm threat for the weekend. With high RH values at \nnight there could be some patchy fog at times this week into the\nweekend. Highly dependent on winds and precipitation though. \nThus have left it out of the forecast for now. With cloudy skies\nthis weekend, lows should be mild limiting the frost potential.",
